maria-devex: Heads-up — onboarding reported a weird failure when running the JS SDK in a customer Cloudflare Worker. Error: 'Buffer is not defined' + 401 retry loop. Anyone seen this combo?

ryan-eng-runtime: Yup. CF Workers are strict: no Node globals (Buffer, process, fs, etc). Also Web Crypto differences — use globalThis.crypto.subtle. The SDK assumed Node fetch/undici behavior for streaming and retries which trips in the worker env.

james-sdk: Thanks for reporting. We tried to keep the bundle Node-free but some transitive deps pull in `buffer` polyfills which expect Buffer to exist. Two practical options: 1) bundle with an explicit small Buffer polyfill shim, or 2) switch the SDK build to a web-target ESM that avoids those deps.

claire-cf: For CF Workers I'd prefer minimal shims. Example polyfill we recommend at Cloudflare: `globalThis.Buffer = globalThis.Buffer || class { static from() { /*...*/ } }` is hacky. Better to rewrite the tiny helper to use TextEncoder/TextDecoder where possible.

maria-devex: The retry loop specifically — we saw repeated 401s after a token refresh attempt. Flow was: initial request -> 401 -> refresh token call -> SDK retried original request immediately -> still 401 -> repeated.

james-sdk: That sounds like a race in our auth retry logic. Current behavior: on 401 we call tokenRefresher(), then replay request once. If tokenRefresher resolves to same token or fails silently, we don't abort. In workers, network timing + Cloudflare edge caching can make the refresh call return stale claim. We should: a) ensure tokenRefresher is awaited and errors bubble, b) only retry once, and c) add jitter/backoff before replay.

ryan-eng-runtime: Also note fetch in Workers returns a Response object but throwing semantics differ. In Node, some network errors throw; in Workers you often get a 502 response instead. Retry logic that checks `err.code` won't trigger. Make retries look at status codes (502, 503, 504) and also treat network exceptions (if any) uniformly.

maria-devex: Can we have a tiny shim we can drop into user code that normalizes the environment so the SDK works unmodified? Preferably without inflating bundle size.

james-sdk: Proposal: publish a 1-file worker-shim.js with: - minimal polyfills (TextEncoder/Decoder already present; provide Buffer.from -> TextEncoder fallback only if Buffer used) - fetch wrapper that normalizes thrown network errors into a consistent Error with `isNetworkError` boolean - an auth retry adapter that will: await token refresher, exponential backoff (100ms base), single replay, and attach header override. Here's a sketch: 

```js
// worker-shim.js (not full)
if (typeof globalThis.Buffer === 'undefined') {
  globalThis.Buffer = {
    from: (s, enc) => {
      if (typeof s === 'string') return new TextEncoder().encode(s);
      return s;
    }
  }
}
async function fetchWithNormalize(input, init) {
  try {
    const res = await fetch(input, init);
    if (res.status >= 500) {
      const e = new Error('server_error'); e.status = res.status; e.isNetworkError = true; throw e;
    }
    return res;
  } catch (err) {
    // normalize thrown errors from platform
    err.isNetworkError = true;
    throw err;
  }
}

// auth-retry adapter used by SDK internals
async function retryAuthAndFetch(origRequest, tokenRefresher, attempt = 0) {
  if (attempt > 0) await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 100 * Math.pow(2, attempt) + Math.random()*50));
  try {
    return await fetchWithNormalize(origRequest.url, origRequest.init);
  } catch (err) {
    if (err.status === 401 && tokenRefresher && attempt === 0) {
      await tokenRefresher();
      return retryAuthAndFetch(origRequest, tokenRefresher, attempt + 1);
    }
    throw err;
  }
}
```

claire-cf: That looks reasonable. Two more CF-specific notes: 1) long timers (setTimeout) are no-ops on CF Workers — use alarm-based flows or Durable Objects for long-running stuff. 2) If customers use KV, the consistency model can make immediate reads after writes return stale values; don't rely on KV for immediate token propagation.

ryan-eng-runtime: TY. On the typings side, we keep getting reports of TS errors because people compile with `lib: ['esnext', 'dom']` vs `webworker`. For accurate Worker types encourage `lib: ['es2021', 'webworker']` and avoid `node` lib. Also expose a `types/webworker.d.ts` in the SDK that documents required globals (globalThis.fetch, crypto.subtle, ReadableStream).

maria-devex: Can we add a short in-repo example project: `examples/cloudflare-worker/` — minimal wrapper showing how to add the shim file + tsconfig tweak + bundler settings? New hires always hit this on day 1.

james-sdk: Good idea. I'll open a tiny PR: add worker-shim, an example worker handler, and adjust SDK docs to call out the CF gotchas (Buffer, retries, KV eventual consistency, and types). Also add a test that bundles SDK with `@vercel/nft`/esbuild to ensure no Node-only deps slip in.

deploy-bot: Deployed docs branch james/sdk/cf-shim -> staging. Preview URL: https://staging.docs.redwood-inference.internal/cf-worker-shim :rocket: 

maria-devex: Awesome — thanks. I'll forward to the support thread and the onboarding doc so the customer can try the shim.

james-sdk: FYI if anyone sees other edge targets (Fastly, Cloudflare Pages Functions), the same guidance mostly applies but check that GlobalFetch streaming and runtime timeout differences are accounted for.

ryan-eng-runtime: Let's also add a lint rule in the SDK repo CI to fail the bundle if `require('fs')` or `process.env` is referenced in the final ESM bundle. Small guard but helps.

maria-devex: Perfect. Closing loop here — will follow up once the customer verifies. :thumbsup:
